Aeromorphic Human Augmentation: A Theoretical Framework for Brain–Computer Interface Control of Supernumerary Wing Extensions

Author: Ouadi Maakoul

---

Abstract

We prove that the maximum aerodynamic lift a human can generate via a non-invasive brain–computer interface is fundamentally limited by neural channel capacity, not by actuator power or wing surface, and we derive the exact information‑theoretic bound. This bound is formalized as a rate‑distortion theorem that links the mutual information of motor imagery EEG to the minimum achievable tracking error of wing kinematics, demonstrating that below a critical information transfer rate (≈100 bits/min) the reconstruction distortion inevitably forces the wing into dynamic stall – regardless of actuator speed.

From this necessity we construct a shared‑autonomy architecture in which a human controls a low‑dimensional subspace of the wing configuration (at most four independent parameters) while a model‑predictive‑control copilot guarantees vestibular safety and aerodynamic efficiency. Safety is enforced by a control barrier function that renders the vestibular‑safe set forward‑invariant; stability of the coupled human‑wing system under delayed aerodynamic forces is proven via a Lyapunov–Krasovskii functional that explicitly accounts for the dynamic‑stall time delay. The augmented body schema is geometrically modelled as a Riemannian submersion that preserves the neurological decoupling between biological limbs and supernumerary wings. A concrete experimental validation protocol – a VR‑based minimum viable experiment – is provided to falsify the central hypothesis with minimal hardware.

The framework establishes the informational ceiling for aeromorphic augmentation and demonstrates that progress requires either a breakthrough in neural interface bandwidth or the acceptance of a permanently shared autonomy, where the machine becomes the cerebellum of the new body, handling coordination while human intent provides the goal.

Keywords: brain–computer interface, supernumerary wings, rate‑distortion theory, Riemannian submersion, control barrier function, Lyapunov–Krasovskii stability, shared autonomy, dynamic stall.

