Chapter 1: Introduction

Every tool‑using species eventually confronts the question: where does the body end? Brain–computer interfaces dissolve the skin boundary: a neural signal that directly commands an external actuator and that receives congruent sensory feedback begins, neurologically, to be incorporated into the body schema. Supernumerary robotics has shown that humans can control an extra robotic limb while using their biological limbs (Lisini Baldi et al., 2017). This thesis asks whether the same logic extends to wings – aerodynamic surfaces that generate sustained lift.

The central engineering obstacle is neither structural nor propulsive; it is informational. The non‑invasive EEG channel that conveys motor imagery from brain to machine has a finite capacity, and the kinematic complexity of flapping flight demands a bandwidth that may exceed that capacity. We therefore hypothesize:

Primary Hypothesis (H1)
The maximum achievable lift in a BCI‑controlled wing system is bounded by the information transfer rate (ITR) of the neural interface, such that for any actuation technology,

> L_{\max} \le L_{\mathrm{auto}} + (L_{\mathrm{full}} - L_{\mathrm{auto}})\left[1 - \exp\left(-\alpha \frac{\mathrm{ITR}}{\mathrm{ITR}_{\mathrm{req}}}\right)\right],
> 

where L_{\mathrm{auto}} is the lift provided by the autonomous copilot alone, L_{\mathrm{full}} is the maximum lift with perfect control, and \alpha>0.

The null hypothesis (H0) asserts that current non‑invasive BCIs already supply sufficient ITR to approach L_{\mathrm{full}}.

Testable predictions

1. Doubling BCI ITR will yield diminishing returns in lift beyond a saturation point.
2. For fixed ITR, increasing the number of independently controlled wing parameters beyond p_{\mathrm{eff}} = \lfloor \mathrm{ITR} / (f_{\mathrm{update}} \log_2(1/\delta))\rfloor does not improve aerodynamic performance.
3. Shared autonomy (AI copilot handling low‑level parameters) outperforms full human control whenever ITR < 100 bits/min.

The framework generalizes to low‑gravity environments; a brief note on off‑world implications appears in future work. The dissertation is organised as follows: Chapter 2 defines the morphological boundary extension. Chapter 3 presents the unsteady aerodynamic model. Chapter 4 formalises the neural decoding bottleneck with rate‑distortion theory. Chapter 5 proves delayed‑stability and vestibular safety. Chapter 6 designs the shared autonomy copilot. Chapter 7 specifies afferent feedback for embodiment. Chapter 8 outlines the minimum viable experimental protocol. Chapters 9–11 discuss failure modes, limitations, and conclusions.

