Chapter 2: System Model and Morphological Boundary Extension

2.1 Notation

Human joint configuration \mathbf{q}_h \in \mathcal{M}_h \subset \mathbb{R}^{20}; wing configuration \mathbf{q}_w \in \mathbb{R}^8 (stroke plane angle, feathering, camber, frequency per wing). Augmented state manifold \mathcal{M}_{h,w} = \mathcal{M}_h \times \mathcal{M}_w. EEG data \mathbf{X}(t) \in \mathbb{R}^{N_c \times T}. BCI decoder f_\theta: \mathbb{R}^{N_c \times T} \to \mathcal{M}_w.

2.2 Morphological Boundary Extension as a Riemannian Submersion

We endow \mathcal{M}_{h,w} with the kinetic‑energy metric g_{h,w} = g_h \oplus g_w where g_h is the inertia tensor of the human body and g_w that of the wings. The BCI decoder defines a smooth map \Phi: \mathcal{M}_h \to \mathcal{M}_{h,w} by \Phi(\mathbf{q}_h) = (\mathbf{q}_h, f_\theta(\mathrm{EEG}(\mathbf{q}_h))). Because the decoder processes neural signals that are themselves generated by the user’s motor intent, the map is not arbitrary; with appropriate training it can be made to respect the product structure.

Proposition 2.1 (Decoupling Submersion).
Suppose the decoder is trained so that motor imagery of biological limbs does not covary with imagery for wings. Then \Phi is an isometric immersion and the projection \pi: \mathcal{M}_{h,w} \to \mathcal{M}_h onto the human factor is a Riemannian submersion. Moreover, the tangent bundle splits orthogonally: T\mathcal{M}_{h,w} = T\mathcal{M}_h \oplus T\mathcal{M}_w, and the Lie bracket of any vector field on the human factor with any vector field on the wing factor vanishes:

[\mathbf{V}_h, \mathbf{V}_w] = 0, \quad \mathbf{V}_h \in \mathfrak{X}(\mathcal{M}_h),\; \mathbf{V}_w \in \mathfrak{X}(\mathcal{M}_w).

Proof. The decoder produces a wing configuration that depends only on the EEG signals classified as wing‑imagery. If the user’s neural manifold for biological limbs and supernumerary limbs are functionally independent (no cross‑talk), the pushforward of a biological motor command under \Phi has zero component in T\mathcal{M}_w. The metric respects the product structure, so horizontal and vertical distributions are orthogonal and the bracket condition follows from the integrability of the product foliation. ∎

This geometric result formalises the neurological requirement that the human can command the wings without involuntarily moving their biological arms; it is the mathematical prerequisite for true embodiment.

2.3 Closed‑Loop Dynamics

The full augmented system is a stochastic delay‑differential‑algebraic system:

\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{M}_w(\mathbf{q}_w)\ddot{\mathbf{q}}_w + \mathbf{C}_w(\mathbf{q}_w,\dot{\mathbf{q}}_w)\dot{\mathbf{q}}_w + \mathbf{G}_w(\mathbf{q}_w) &= \boldsymbol{\tau}_w + \mathbf{J}_{\mathrm{aero}}^{\mathsf{T}} \mathbf{F}_{\mathrm{aero}}(\mathbf{q}_w,\dot{\mathbf{q}}_w,\mathbf{w}_{\mathrm{wind}}, t),\\
\dot{\mathbf{q}}_w(t) &= g(\mathbf{u}_w(t-\tau_d)),\\
\mathbf{u}_w(t) &= f_\theta(\mathbf{X}(t)),
\end{aligned}

with neural delay \tau_d \approx 100 ms, aerodynamic delay \tau_{\mathrm{stall}} (Chapter 3), and measurement noise.

