Chapter 3: Wing Morphology and Unsteady Aerodynamics

3.1 Kinematic Parameterisation

Each wing: stroke angle \phi_s \in [-\pi/2,\pi/2], feathering \phi_f \in [-\pi/4,\pi/4], camber \kappa \in [0,1], frequency \omega \in [0,\omega_{\max}]. Total \mathbf{q}_w \in \mathbb{R}^8.

3.2 Unsteady Blade Element Theory (UBET)

Because flapping occurs at reduced frequencies k = \frac{\omega c}{2U} \sim 1, quasi‑steady aerodynamics fail. We incorporate:

· Added mass: force proportional to acceleration of surrounding fluid.
· Dynamic stall: delayed separation modelled by a first‑order lag in effective angle of attack:
  \tau_{\mathrm{stall}} \dot{\alpha}_{\mathrm{eff}} + \alpha_{\mathrm{eff}} = \alpha(t),
  with \tau_{\mathrm{stall}} \approx 0.1 s.
· Wake capture: accounted via an empirical enhancement factor \Gamma = 1.2 on the lift coefficient after stroke reversal.

Local velocity at span station s:

v(s,t) = \dot{\phi}_s(t) s + v_{\mathrm{gust}} + v_{\mathrm{ind}}.

Lift per unit span:

dL(s,t) = \frac12 \rho v^2 c(s) C_L(\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}, \kappa)\, ds + \frac{\pi}{4}\rho c(s)^2 \bigl(\dot{v}_\perp + \dot{\alpha}_{\mathrm{eff}} \tfrac{c(s)}{2}\bigr) ds.

3.3 Aerodynamic Feasibility under Unsteady Enhancement

Proposition 3.1 (Dynamic Stall Multiplier).
For a sinusoidally pitching wing near stall, the cycle‑averaged lift coefficient \overline{C}_L can be up to three times the static maximum C_{L,\max} due to dynamic stall (Dickinson et al., 1999). Hence the required flapping frequency for a given lift obeys

\omega_{\mathrm{req}} \le \sqrt{\frac{2mg}{\rho R^3 S \, \overline{C}_L}},

with \overline{C}_L \approx 1.5–2.5 achievable under appropriate feathering.

For a 70 kg human with wing half‑span R = 1.5 m, area S = 4 m^2, the unsteady‑assisted frequency is \omega_{\mathrm{req}} \le 2.8 Hz, well within electromechanical feasibility.

Off‑Earth scaling. A short scaling plot (Figure 3.1) shows required wing area as a function of gravity and atmospheric density. On Mars (\rho = 0.02 kg/m³, g = 3.7 m/s²), sustained flight would demand S > 40 m² at 10 Hz, which is beyond current actuator and structural limits. Therefore this thesis restricts itself to Earth atmospheric conditions; a full treatment of off‑world environments is deferred to future work.

