Chapter 5: Stability and Safety under Delayed Feedback

5.1 Vestibular Constraints and Control Barrier Functions

The human vestibular system tolerates sustained linear acceleration |\mathbf{a}| \le A_{\max} = 0.3g and angular velocity |\boldsymbol{\Omega}| \le \Omega_{\max} = 0.5 rad/s. Define the safe set:

\mathcal{C} = \{ \mathbf{x} = [\mathbf{q}_w^\mathsf{T},\,\dot{\mathbf{q}}_w^\mathsf{T}]^\mathsf{T} \in \mathbb{R}^{16} \mid h(\mathbf{x}) = \min_i (a_{i,\max} - |\mathbf{J}_{\mathrm{vest},i} \dot{\mathbf{q}}_w|) \ge 0 \}.

To guarantee that no user command ever drives the system out of \mathcal{C}, the AI copilot enforces a Control Barrier Function (CBF). For the affine control system \dot{\mathbf{x}} = \mathbf{f}(\mathbf{x}) + \mathbf{g}(\mathbf{x}) \mathbf{u}, the CBF condition is:

\sup_{\mathbf{u}} \bigl[ L_{\mathbf{f}} h(\mathbf{x}) + L_{\mathbf{g}} h(\mathbf{x}) \mathbf{u} + \lambda h(\mathbf{x}) \bigr] \ge 0, \quad \lambda > 0.

Theorem 5.1 (Forward Invariance under CBF–MPC).
Let the wing control input \boldsymbol{\tau}_w be the solution of a model predictive control problem that includes the CBF inequality as a hard constraint. Then, for any bounded human intent signal, the closed‑loop trajectory satisfies \mathbf{x}(t) \in \mathcal{C} for all t \ge 0, provided \mathbf{x}(0) \in \mathcal{C}. In particular, vestibular overload is mathematically impossible.

Proof. Nagumo’s theorem: the CBF condition ensures \dot{h} + \lambda h \ge 0, so h stays non‑negative. ∎

5.2 Harness Uncertainty Bound

The vestibular Jacobian \mathbf{J}_{\mathrm{vest}} depends on the mechanical coupling between wings and torso. Soft‑tissue compliance introduces an additive uncertainty \Delta \mathbf{J}_{\mathrm{vest}} with \|\Delta \mathbf{J}_{\mathrm{vest}}\|_2 \le \epsilon. We require that the CBF be robust to this uncertainty. A sufficient condition is:

Proposition 5.2 (Robust CBF).
If the nominal CBF constraint is tightened by a margin \epsilon \| \dot{\mathbf{q}}_w \|, i.e., h(\mathbf{x}) \ge \epsilon \|\dot{\mathbf{q}}_w\|, then the true system remains safe under any \Delta \mathbf{J}_{\mathrm{vest}} of norm \le \epsilon. The hardware design must therefore ensure a harness stiffness such that \epsilon is below this margin.

5.3 Delayed Input‑to‑State Stability (ISS)

The aerodynamic model contains a time delay \tau_{\mathrm{stall}} in the dynamic stall dynamics. Standard Lyapunov functions for ODEs are insufficient; we employ Lyapunov–Krasovskii functionals.

Let the wing tracking error be \mathbf{e}_w(t) = \mathbf{q}_w(t) - \mathbf{q}_{w,\mathrm{des}}(t). The error dynamics are a retarded functional differential equation with delay \tau = \tau_{\mathrm{stall}}:

\mathbf{M}_w \ddot{\mathbf{e}}_w + \mathbf{C}_w \dot{\mathbf{e}}_w + \mathbf{K}_p \mathbf{e}_w + \mathbf{K}_d \dot{\mathbf{e}}_w = \boldsymbol{\Delta}_{\mathrm{aero}}(\mathbf{e}_w(t), \mathbf{e}_w(t-\tau)) + \mathbf{w}(t).

Theorem 5.3 (ISS under Delayed Aerodynamic Uncertainty).
Assume there exist positive definite matrices \mathbf{P}, \mathbf{Q}, \mathbf{R} such that the Lyapunov–Krasovskii functional

V(\mathbf{e}_w,\dot{\mathbf{e}}_w,\mathbf{e}_{w,t}) = \frac12 \dot{\mathbf{e}}_w^\mathsf{T} \mathbf{M}_w \dot{\mathbf{e}}_w + \frac12 \mathbf{e}_w^\mathsf{T} \mathbf{K}_p \mathbf{e}_w + \int_{-\tau}^{0} \mathbf{e}_w(t+\theta)^\mathsf{T} \mathbf{Q} \mathbf{e}_w(t+\theta)\,d\theta

satisfies \dot{V} \le -\lambda_{\min}(\mathbf{K}_d) \|\dot{\mathbf{e}}_w\|^2 + \gamma \|\boldsymbol{\Delta}_{\mathrm{aero}}\|^2 along trajectories, with \gamma>0. Then the error dynamics are input‑to‑state stable with respect to the disturbance \boldsymbol{\Delta}_{\mathrm{aero}}. That is, there exist \beta \in \mathcal{KL} and \gamma_\infty \in \mathcal{K} such that

\|[\mathbf{e}_w^\mathsf{T},\dot{\mathbf{e}}_w^\mathsf{T}]^\mathsf{T}\| \le \beta(\|\mathbf{x}(0)\|, t) + \gamma_\infty(\sup_{s \le t} \|\boldsymbol{\Delta}_{\mathrm{aero}}(s)\|).

Proof sketch. Compute \dot{V}, apply the Leibniz rule to the integral term, and use Young’s inequality on cross‑terms. The delay introduces an additional term -\mathbf{e}_w(t-\tau)^\mathsf{T}\mathbf{Q}\mathbf{e}_w(t-\tau) which aids dissipation. ∎

This theorem guarantees that the wing controller remains stable even though the true aerodynamic forces lag the model by up to \tau.

