Chapter 6: Shared Autonomy as a Cerebellar Copilot

6.1 Necessity of Shared Autonomy

Theorem 4.1 forces the conclusion: direct human control of all 8 wing DOFs is impossible with current BCI rates. We therefore adopt a hierarchical architecture:

· Human layer (intent): Decodes 4 high‑level parameters at 4 Hz: net lift vector sagittal projection, lift magnitude, heading rate, mode selector.
· AI copilot layer (execution): Maps these 4 intents to the full 8 wing kinematic setpoints at 50 Hz via MPC, while satisfying the CBF safety constraint.

The blending between human and autonomous command is governed by a dynamic trust factor \rho(t):

\tau \dot{\rho} = -\rho + \rho_{\mathrm{ss}}(C(t)), \quad \rho_{\mathrm{ss}}(C) = \frac{1}{1+\exp(\kappa(C_{\mathrm{th}} - C))},

where C(t) is the estimated BCI channel capacity (ITR) computed online from the decoder confidence, \tau = 0.2 s is a time constant ensuring \dot{\rho} varies slower than the wing dynamics, and \kappa adjusts the slope of the transition. The final wing velocity command is:

\dot{\mathbf{q}}_{w,\mathrm{final}} = \rho(t)\, \mathbf{M}_{\mathrm{expand}} \mathbf{u}_w(t) + (1-\rho(t))\, \dot{\mathbf{q}}_{w,\mathrm{auto}}(t).

6.2 MPC Copilot with CBF

The copilot solves the following optimisation at 50 Hz:

\begin{aligned}
\min_{\dot{\mathbf{q}}_w^{0:N-1}} & \sum_{k=0}^{N-1} \Bigl( \|\mathbf{F}_{\mathrm{des}}(\mathbf{u}_w) - \mathbf{F}_{\mathrm{aero}}^k\|_Q^2 + \|\dot{\mathbf{q}}_w^k\|_R^2 \Bigr) \\
\text{s.t.} \quad & \text{kinematics, joint limits}, \\
& L_{\mathbf{f}} h(\mathbf{x}^k) + L_{\mathbf{g}} h(\mathbf{x}^k) \mathbf{u}^k + \lambda h(\mathbf{x}^k) \ge 0 \quad \forall k,
\end{aligned}

which is a quadratic program solved in < 5 ms on an edge GPU.

6.3 Fallback Protocols

If the BCI ITR drops below 10 bits/min for more than 2 s, \rho is forced to 0 and an auditory “autonomy engaged” alert sounds. Recovery requires a dedicated calibration gesture.

