Appendix B – Proof of the Rate‑Distortion Bound (Theorem 4.1)

Theorem. Let the BCI channel operate with capacity  C  bits per update at frequency  f_s  Hz. For the intended feathering angle  \alpha(t)  modelled as a zero‑mean Gaussian process with variance  \sigma_\alpha^2 , the reconstructed angle  \hat{\alpha}_k  must satisfy

\mathbb{E}[(\alpha_k - \hat{\alpha}_k)^2] \ge \sigma_\alpha^2 \, 2^{-2C/f_s} .

If this minimum distortion exceeds  D_{\max} = (15^\circ)^2 , then the RMS tracking error forces the wing into dynamic stall with non‑zero probability.

Proof.
The discretised motor intent  \alpha_k  is transmitted across a channel of capacity  C  bits per second. With an update rate  f_s , the capacity per sample is  C/f_s  bits. The information‑transmission inequality gives

I(\alpha_k ; \hat{\alpha}_k) \ge R(D_k) ,

where  D_k = \mathbb{E}[(\alpha_k - \hat{\alpha}_k)^2] . For a Gaussian source the rate‑distortion function is  R(D) = \frac12 \log_2 (\sigma_\alpha^2/D)  when  D \le \sigma_\alpha^2 . Since the channel limits the mutual information to at most  C/f_s , we have

\frac12 \log_2 \frac{\sigma_\alpha^2}{D_k} \le \frac{C}{f_s} \implies D_k \ge \sigma_\alpha^2 \, 2^{-2C/f_s}.

Now stall occurs when the true angle of attack deviates from the optimum by more than  \theta_{\mathrm{stall}} = 15^\circ . Choosing  D_{\max} = \theta_{\mathrm{stall}}^2 , the condition  D_k > D_{\max}  implies that the tracking error exceeds the stall margin. When  C < \frac{f_s}{2} \log_2(\sigma_\alpha^2 / D_{\max}) , the lower bound on distortion strictly exceeds  D_{\max} . Consequently, no coding scheme can keep the error within the safe range; dynamic stall is inevitable in finite time. ∎

