Appendix D – Optimal User‑Controlled Subspace (Section 4.3)

The four user‑controlled parameters are chosen to maximise aerodynamic authority while limiting vestibular disturbance. Let  S  be an orthonormal basis for a  p -dimensional subspace of the wing velocity space  \mathbb{R}^8  ( p=4 ). The user issues a command  \mathbf{v} \in S , and the full wing velocity is  \dot{\mathbf{q}}_w = \mathbf{M}_{\mathrm{expand}} \mathbf{v} .

We define the aerodynamic gain  \sigma_{\min}(\mathbf{J}_{\mathrm{aero}} S) , where  \mathbf{J}_{\mathrm{aero}} : \mathbb{R}^8 \to \mathbb{R}^6  maps wing velocity to force/moment. We wish to maximise the minimum singular value (the weakest control direction) under the constraint that the vestibular gain is bounded:

\| \mathbf{J}_{\mathrm{vest}} S \|_2 \le \delta.

This is a non‑convex problem over the Stiefel manifold. A computationally efficient approach is to compute the generalized singular value decomposition (GSVD) of the pair  (\mathbf{J}_{\mathrm{aero}}, \mathbf{J}_{\mathrm{vest}}) . Let

\mathbf{J}_{\mathrm{aero}} = \mathbf{U} \boldsymbol{\Sigma}_a \mathbf{R}, \qquad \mathbf{J}_{\mathrm{vest}} = \mathbf{V} \boldsymbol{\Sigma}_v \mathbf{R},

with  \mathbf{R}  invertible (square  8\times 8 ). The columns of  \mathbf{R}^{-1}  correspond to directions that simultaneously diagonalise the two operators. The generalized singular values are  \sigma_i = \sigma_{a,i} / \sigma_{v,i} . The optimal subspace  S  is spanned by the four columns of  \mathbf{R}^{-1}  with the largest  \sigma_i  that also satisfy  \sigma_{v,i} \le \delta . This yields the maximal force‑to‑vestibular ratio, guaranteeing intuitive control. A rigorous proof of optimality is given in (Zhou et al., 1996).

