Appendix E – Unsteady Blade Element Model: Full Derivation

E.1 Coordinate Frames
The wing kinematic chain defines a local frame  \{L\}  at each blade element. The velocity of a blade element at span  s  in the inertial frame is

\mathbf{v}_I = \dot{\mathbf{r}}_{I/B} + \boldsymbol{\omega}_{B/I} \times \mathbf{r}_{B} + \boldsymbol{\omega}_{L/B} \times (s\,\mathbf{e}_y) + \dot{\delta} \,\mathbf{e}_z,

where  \boldsymbol{\omega}_{L/B} = \dot{\phi}_s \mathbf{e}_x + \dot{\phi}_f \mathbf{e}_y .

E.2 Added Mass
For a thin wing section, the added mass force per unit span normal to the chord (the  z -direction in the local frame) is

dF_{\mathrm{added}} = \frac{\pi}{4} \rho c(s)^2 \bigl( \ddot{z}_{\mathrm{local}} + \dot{\alpha}_{\mathrm{eff}} \frac{c(s)}{2} \bigr) ds,

where  z_{\mathrm{local}}  is the normal displacement, and  \alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}  is the effective angle of attack including dynamic stall lag (E.3). This expression follows from classical unsteady thin‑airfoil theory (Theodorsen, 1935) simplified for fast flapping.

E.3 Dynamic Stall
The dynamic stall model employs a single‑lag approximation for the effective angle of attack:

\tau_{\mathrm{stall}} \dot{\alpha}_{\mathrm{eff}} + \alpha_{\mathrm{eff}} = \alpha_{\mathrm{geom}},

with  \tau_{\mathrm{stall}} = 0.1  s (based on a reduced frequency  k \approx 1 ). The static lift coefficient is modified by a stall‑delay factor  \psi(\dot{\alpha}) = \max(1, 1 + 1.5 \dot{\alpha} c/(2U)) , giving

C_L(\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}, \dot{\alpha}) = \psi \cdot C_{L,\mathrm{static}}(\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}).

During stroke reversal, an additional wake‑capture increment  \Gamma = 1.2  is added to account for recirculating vorticity (Dickinson et al., 1999). Thus the total lift per unit span is

dL = \frac12 \rho v^2 c(s) \, C_L(\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}, \kappa) \, ds + dF_{\mathrm{added}}^\perp .

E.4 Parameter Values for Prototype
Wing half‑span  R = 1.5  m, chord distribution  c(s) = 0.5 (1 - s/R)  m; total area  S = 4  m². Density  \rho = 1.225  kg/m³. Flapping frequency range 0–5 Hz. Maximum stroke amplitude  \pm 45^\circ , feathering  \pm 45^\circ . Structural mass per wing 3 kg, moment of inertia about root 0.8 kg m².

