Appendix H – Experimental Validation Protocols

H.1 Minimum Viable Experiment (Year 1)

· Participants: 10 healthy adults, no prior BCI experience, 20 sessions of 2‑class motor imagery (hand vs. foot) over 4 weeks.
· EEG: 64‑channel wet cap, g.USBamp, 512 Hz. Riemannian covariance features, MDM classifier.
· VR Environment: Virtual landscape with a first‑person wing avatar. The user controls the lift vector direction on the sagittal plane (up/forward) via the BCI; rest is autonomous.
· Metrics: ITR (bits/min), tracking error (RMS angle difference between desired and achieved lift vector), number of virtual stall events (deviation > 15°).
· Statistical test: Mixed‑effects model of tracking error vs. ITR; hypothesised negative exponential relationship, plateau at high ITR. Falsification if RMSE drops below 15° for ITR < 60 bits/min.

H.2 Wind‑Tunnel Validation
A 1:2 scale wing prototype with 8 servo actuators will be mounted on a 6‑axis force/torque sensor in a low‑speed wind tunnel (10 m/s). Sinusoidal flapping with varying frequency and feathering will be commanded, and forces compared to UBET predictions. Dynamic stall parameters  \tau_{\mathrm{stall}}, \Gamma  will be identified via least‑squares fit.

H.3 Shared Autonomy VR Benchmark
Same VR setup but with 4‑parameter BCI (as per optimal subspace) versus full autonomy versus direct human (full 8‑DOF attempted). A dual‑task paradigm (verbal n‑back) will simulate cognitive load variations. The trust factor  \rho(t)  will be modulated by real‑time ITR estimates; the experiment will record vestibular violation rate, lift efficiency, and subjective workload (NASA‑TLX).

